<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sierra Leone</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sierra Leone is a country on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. The Carthaginian explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanno-the-navigator">Hanno the Navigator</a>'s voyage around 500 BCE may have reached as far as modern Sierra Leone or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. The interpretation of his surviving account is debated by scholars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sierra-leone.jpg" alt="Sierra Leone" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sierra_Leone.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sierra Leone lies on the south-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> to the north and east, Liberia to the south-east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the west. It has about 400 km of coastline. The east is a plateau with high mountains, where Mount Bintumani reaches 1,948 m, and lowland plains of forest, bush and farmland cover about 43% of the land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> stands on a mountainous coastal peninsula. The name comes from the Lion Mountains near Freetown, which the Portuguese explorer Pedro de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sintra">Sintra</a> named Serra Leoa, meaning lioness mountains, in 1462.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited for at least 2,500 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">Iron technology</a> was adopted by the 9th century and agriculture established on the coast by about 1000 CE. Dense rainforest and swamps, and the tsetse fly, whose disease killed the horses and zebu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> of the Mandé people, protected the region against invasion by the Mandinka Empire and limited the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. Susu traders brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 18th century.</p><p>European contact began in the 15th century. Traders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> set up stations that came to deal mainly in slaves, brought to the coast by traders from the interior. In 1808 the coastal Sierra Leone Colony was founded as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, to resettle Africans returning after the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and in 1896 an inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> was created. Independence came in 1961 under Prime Minister Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-margai">Milton Margai</a>. A constitution of 1971 made the country a presidential republic under Siaka Stevens. In 1991 a civil war began between the government and the Revolutionary United Front. It lasted 11 years and ended in 2002 after intervention by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecomog">ECOMOG</a> forces and then the United Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> is the capital and largest city. The country is a presidential republic, and English is the official language, while Krio, derived from English and several African languages, is spoken across the country. About 18 ethnic groups live there, the Temne and Mende being the largest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> people descend from freed African-American and Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> slaves and liberated Africans. Bunce Island, near Freetown, holds the ruins of a slave fortress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sierra Leone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1896, in the Fourth Anglo-Ashanti War, a British expedition occupied Kumasi without a fight and deposed Asantehene Prempeh I, who was exiled first to <b>Sierra Leone</b> and from 1900 to the Seychelles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Fante and the Ga of the new town of Accra looked to the British for protection, and in 1821 the Crown dissolved the company and gave the forts to Charles MacCarthy, governor of <b>Sierra Leone</b>, with a mandate to impose peace and end the slave trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The civil wars of 1989–2003 destabilised the whole region, spreading into <b>Sierra Leone</b>, and the conviction of Charles Taylor in 2012 by the Special Court for Sierra Leone was the first conviction of a former head of state by an international tribunal since Nuremberg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Atlantic coast of Africa, probably in the fifth century BCE, and may have reached as far as <b>Sierra Leone</b> or even Cameroon according to competing interpretations of his surviving periplus; it was the most ambitious known exploration of that coast before the Portuguese voyages of the fifteenth…</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Black Christians took heart from the verse of the Psalms “Ethiopia shall soon stretch out her hands unto God”, and some settled in <b>Sierra Leone</b>, where Freetown was founded in 1792, or in Liberia, colonised from 1822 by the American Colonization Society and independent from 1847.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…rule across Africa and the Pacific, and many states after independence gave it a formal place: Botswana’s Ntlo ya Dikgosi, the House of Chiefs, Ghana’s National House of Chiefs and South Africa’s houses of traditional leaders advise elected governments, and <b>Sierra Leone</b>’s local government rests on its chiefdoms.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
